      <span class="pulse"></span>
      Automation pipeline ready for paid onboarding
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="metrics" aria-label="Strategic metrics">
  <div><strong>50-85%</strong><span>Target infra cost reduction vs generic cloud, subject to workload</span></div>
  <div><strong>30-50%</strong><span>Benchmark target for throughput uplift in suitable bare-metal cases</span></div>
  <div><strong>99.9%</strong><span>Pro SLA target after paid deployment</span></div>
  <div><strong>99.99%</strong><span>Enterprise SLA target with redundancy design</span></div>
</section>

<section id="problem" class="section problem-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Market Problem</p>
    <h2>Web3 infrastructure is too expensive, too abstracted, and too risky when uptime drives revenue.</h2>
    <p>Validators and node operators do not only buy servers. They buy continuity, block participation, latency, and confidence that operations will not fail at the worst moment.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pillar-grid">
    <div><strong>Cloud cost pressure</strong><p>Generic cloud is convenient, but high-volume node workloads can become structurally expensive as request, storage, and bandwidth usage grows.</p></div>
    <div><strong>Slashing exposure</strong><p>Validator downtime can create protocol penalties. GSVIEC positions infrastructure around uptime visibility, failover thinking, and clear SLA boundaries.</p></div>
    <div><strong>Limited control</strong><p>RPC and managed cloud services are fast to start, but teams lose hardware control, tuning depth, and direct ownership of critical operations.</p></div>
    <div><strong>Manual DevOps burden</strong><p>Dedicated servers are cheaper but difficult to operate without automation, monitoring, security policy, and engineer-level support.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="services" class="section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Core Infrastructure</p>
    <h2>A focused stack for node operators, DePIN teams, and enterprise blockchain builders.</h2>
    <p>The public offer is narrow by design: bare-metal automation, validator operations, private Web3 cloud, and DevOps automation.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="service-grid">
    {% for item in services %}
    <a class="service-card" href="/services/{{ item.slug }}/">
      <span>0{{ loop.index }}</span>
      <h3>{{ item.title }}</h3>
      <p>{{ item.summary }}</p>
      <small>{{ item.outcome }}</small>
    </a>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
</section>

<section id="plans" class="section plans-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Commercial Model</p>
    <h2>Three offers that turn technical interest into paid infrastructure.</h2>
    <p>Pricing below is indicative and should be confirmed after node requirements, chain, region, redundancy, bandwidth, and support level are scoped.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="pricing-grid">
    <article class="pricing-card">
      <span>Starter</span>
      <h3>Open-source automation</h3>
      <strong>Free</strong>
      <p>Scripts and documentation for technical teams that want to run a node on their own server.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Ansible/Terraform starter repo</li>
        <li>Local or customer-owned server</li>
        <li>Community-driven lead capture</li>
      </ul>
      <a class="button secondary" href="#consultation">Request repo access</a>
    </article>
    <article class="pricing-card featured">
      <span>Pro</span>
      <h3>Managed single node</h3>
      <strong>$100-200/node/mo</strong>
      <p>Paid node deployment with monitoring, basic support, and a target SLA for early operators.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Automated bare-metal deployment</li>
        <li>Monitoring and uptime reports</li>
        <li>14-day refund policy after upfront payment</li>
      </ul>
      <a class="button primary" href="#consultation">Start Pro scope</a>
    </article>
    <article class="pricing-card">
      <span>Enterprise</span>
      <h3>Multi-node failover</h3>
      <strong>$500+/mo</strong>
      <p>Custom topology for validators, DePIN projects, and enterprise blockchain systems.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Multi-node redundancy</li>
        <li>Auto-failover beta path</li>
        <li>24/7 technical support and SLA 99.99% target</li>
      </ul>
      <a class="button secondary" href="#consultation">Book enterprise demo</a>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="architecture" class="section split-section">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Infrastructure Architecture</p>
    <h2>From physical servers to repeatable Web3 operations.</h2>
    <p>GSVIEC's strategic advantage is the bridge between bare-metal control and software-defined operations. The system should make a dedicated server behave like a disciplined infrastructure product.</p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li>PXE boot and automated OS provisioning for fast node setup</li>
      <li>BIOS, firmware, VLAN, and bonding policy for performance control</li>
      <li>Terraform and Ansible scripts for repeatable deployments</li>
      <li>Monitoring, alerts, backup, and failover readiness for uptime</li>
      <li>KYC-light and paid onboarding to reduce abuse risk</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="architecture-panel">
    <div>Customer KYC</div>
    <div>Paid Scope</div>
    <div>Terraform</div>
    <div>Ansible</div>
    <div>PXE Boot</div>
    <div>Node Runtime</div>
    <div>Prometheus</div>
    <div>Failover</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section blockchain-section">
  <div class="blockchain-copy">
    <p class="eyebrow">Proof of Technical Direction</p>
    <h2>Open-source scripts create trust; managed operations create revenue.</h2>
    <p>The Starter repo attracts technical operators. Pro and Enterprise convert teams that need SLA, support, failover, and someone accountable for production readiness.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="block-card" aria-label="Validator node status">
    <span>Validator Node</span>
    <strong>Healthy</strong>
    <code>slot_lag: 0</code>
    <code>latency: monitored</code>
    <small>Uptime, alerting, and failover reports available after deployment</small>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="comparison" class="section comparison-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Competitive Position</p>
    <h2>Between DIY dedicated servers and expensive enterprise node services.</h2>
    <p>GSVIEC should compete on control, support, and price-performance: more accountable than pure DIY, more flexible than RPC-only providers, and more accessible than high-end enterprise vendors.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="comparison-table" role="table" aria-label="Competitive comparison">
    <div role="row" class="table-head"><span>Option</span><span>Strength</span><span>Tradeoff</span><span>GSVIEC angle</span></div>
    <div role="row"><span>Infura / Alchemy</span><span>Fast API access</span><span>No hardware control; volume-based cost</span><span>Better for teams needing owned node operations</span></div>
    <div role="row"><span>Chainstack</span><span>Managed multi-chain access</span><span>Request-based scaling economics</span><span>More control for performance-sensitive workloads</span></div>
    <div role="row"><span>Akash</span><span>Decentralized cloud economics</span><span>Token and stability uncertainty</span><span>SLA-led operating model with technical support</span></div>
    <div role="row"><span>Hetzner / OVH DIY</span><span>Low server cost</span><span>Customer handles all DevOps risk</span><span>Automation, monitoring, and Web3-specific operations</span></div>
    <div role="row"><span>Blockdaemon</span><span>Enterprise node service</span><span>Premium pricing</span><span>Focused middle position for startups and growing infra teams</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section trust-section">
